1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
The sun has gone down. So why does this street still feel like an oven?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Because a city is covered in materials that soak up sunlight.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Roads, roofs and walls absorb energy through the day, then release some of that stored heat after sunset.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
And where have the trees gone?

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Vegetation cools its surroundings with shade and by moving water from soil and leaves into the air.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Replace much of that greenery with hard, dry surfaces, and you lose some of that cooling.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
So every street is equally hot?

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Not at all. A shaded park, a tree-lined street and a wide paved car park can feel very different.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Building layout and airflow matter too.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 01:00:00,000
Vehicles, buildings and air-conditioning systems can add waste heat as well.

11
01:00:00,000 --> 01:08:000
The result is an urban heat island: built-up places can be warmer than nearby less-developed areas.

12
01:08:000 --> 01:11:000
Can we make cities cooler?

13
01:11:000 --> 01:22:000
We can help. More shade and vegetation, greener roofs, and more reflective roofs and pavements can all reduce heat in the right conditions.

14
01:22:000 --> 01:25:000
The best choice depends on the place.

15
01:25:000 --> 01:30:000
So the heat isn't just in the weather. It's also in what we've built.
